 {"id": "1.1", "title": "Arithmetic Operators Overview",
  "concept": "Python has seven arithmetic operators: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, floor division, modulo, and exponentiation. Most of them behave as you expect. Two deserve special attention. First, regular division always returns a float, even when the numbers divide evenly. That is a deliberate change that arrived with Python three, because Python two used to chop off the decimals, and many programmers were surprised by it. Second, the double star is exponentiation, and it is evaluated from right to left. An interesting bonus: the plus operator also joins strings and lists together, and the star operator repeats them, so the same symbols can mean different things depending on the type.",
  "walk": "First, we store ten in a and three in b, on a single line. Addition gives thirteen, and subtraction gives seven. Multiplication gives thirty. Regular division gives three point three, three, three, and so on, which is a float. Notice the tiny error at the very end of that number, because computers store decimals in binary, so some fractions can only be approximated. Floor division, written with two slashes, throws away the fractional part and gives three. Modulo gives the remainder, which is one, because three goes into ten three times, with one left over. And exponentiation, written with two stars, gives ten to the power of three, which is one thousand."},
 {"id": "1.2", "title": "Modulo Operator",
  "concept": "The modulo operator returns the remainder of a division, and it is one of the most useful tools in a programmer's belt. Think of a clock: after twelve comes one again. That wrap-around is modulo at work. Programmers use it to test whether a number is even or odd, to extract the last digit of a number, to cycle through a list again and again, and to solve the famous FizzBuzz interview question. One detail that surprises people who come from C or Java: in Python, the result of modulo takes the sign of the right-hand number. So negative seven modulo three gives two, not negative one. That makes it friendlier for cycling through lists.",
  "walk": "We start with num equal to seventeen. Seventeen modulo two gives one, so the remainder is not zero, and the next line confirms that seventeen is not even. Seventeen modulo ten gives seven, which is simply the last digit of the number. Finally, the loop prints i modulo three for i from zero to five, and you can see the pattern zero, one, two, zero, one, two, repeating forever."},
 {"id": "2.1", "title": "Comparison Operators Overview",
  "concept": "Comparison operators compare two values and always answer with True or False. There are six: equal to, not equal to, greater than, less than, greater than or equal to, and less than or equal to. The most common beginner mistake is to confuse the double equals, which asks a question, with the single equals, which stores a value. Strings can be compared too, and Python compares them letter by letter using their Unicode values, so lowercase letters are considered greater than uppercase ones. One more professional tip: never compare floats with double equals when they come from calculations. Zero point one plus zero point two is not exactly zero point three, so use the math dot isclose function instead.",
  "walk": "We set x to five and y to ten. Equal to gives False, because five is not ten. Not equal to gives True. Greater than gives False, and less than gives True. Greater than or equal to gives False, and less than or equal to gives True."},
 {"id": "2.2", "title": "Chaining Comparisons",
  "concept": "Here is the answer to the puzzle from the beginning. Python lets you chain comparisons, so one less than x less than ten means exactly what it says in mathematics. In C, the same expression is read as one less than x, which gives zero or one, and then that tiny number is compared with ten, which is always true. A famous bug, and Python makes it impossible. Chaining is also more efficient, because x is evaluated only once, which matters when x is a function call. And it short-circuits, so as soon as one link in the chain is false, Python stops checking.",
  "walk": "Here x is five. One less than x less than ten is True, because five sits between one and ten, and notice that we only wrote x once. The second chain checks that x is between zero and ten, inclusive, and it is also True. The last line shows the long way to write the same thing, with the word and, and the answer is identical, but the chained version is shorter and easier to read."},
 {"id": "3.1", "title": "Logical Operators Overview",
  "concept": "Logical operators combine conditions. And is True only when both sides are true. Or is True when at least one side is true. And not flips a value from True to False, or the other way around. Now for a secret that many tutorials skip: and and or do not always return True or False. They return one of their actual operands. So the expression empty string or the word default gives you the word default, and that is a very popular way to supply fallback values. They also short-circuit: in an and, if the left side is false, Python never even looks at the right side. Their priority order is: not first, then and, then or.",
  "walk": "We set a to True and b to False. A and b is False, because and needs both sides to be true. A or b is True, because or needs only one. Not a flips True into False. Now we combine real comparisons: x greater than three and y less than twenty is True, because both parts are true. And x greater than ten or y greater than five is also True, because the second part is true."},
 {"id": "4.1", "title": "Assignment Operators Overview",
  "concept": "The assignment operator stores a value in a variable, and the augmented assignment operators combine an operation with the store. Instead of writing x equals x plus three, you write x plus-equals three. It is shorter, clearer, and very common inside loops. There is a subtle point for lists: plus-equals on a list changes the original list in place, while x equals x plus another list builds a brand new one. Also watch the types. Slash-equals always produces a float, so a whole number becomes a decimal number after it. And since Python three point eight, there is even a walrus operator, written colon-equals, which assigns a value in the middle of an expression.",
  "walk": "We start with x equal to ten and print it. Plus-equals three adds three to x, so x becomes thirteen. Minus-equals five brings it down to eight. Times-equals two doubles it to sixteen. Slash-equals four divides by four, and notice that x is now four point zero, a float, because regular division always returns a float. Floor-divide-equals two gives two point zero. And star-star-equals two squares it, giving four point zero."},
 {"id": "5.1", "title": "Bitwise Operators Overview",
  "concept": "Bitwise operators work directly on the binary digits of integers. They are used in graphics, in network programming, for example to apply a subnet mask to an I-P address, in cryptography, and in data compression. There are six: and, or, exclusive or, not, left shift, and right shift. A helpful rule: shifting left by one position multiplies a number by two, and shifting right by one divides it by two, and that is much faster than ordinary arithmetic in low-level languages. And a curious fact: the not operator, the tilde, turns a number a into negative a minus one. That is the two's complement representation, which is how computers store negative numbers.",
  "walk": "We take a equal to six, which is one, one, zero in binary, and b equal to three, which is zero, one, one. The bin function lets us see both numbers as binary, written with the prefix zero b. Bitwise and keeps only the bits that are set in both numbers, giving two. Bitwise or keeps the bits set in either number, giving seven. Exclusive or, written with a caret, keeps the bits that differ, giving five. The tilde flips every bit, and for a it gives negative seven. Shifting left by one doubles the number, giving twelve, and shifting right by one halves it, giving three."},
 {"id": "5.2", "title": "Practical Use: Flags",
  "concept": "Here is a real-world use of bitwise operators: flags. Each option is assigned a power of two, so that each one occupies its own bit. You can then store many yes-or-no settings in a single number. This is exactly how Unix file permissions work. When you type chmod seven fifty-five, each digit is a combination of read, which is four, write, which is two, and execute, which is one. Python itself uses the same trick in its enum dot Flag class, and in the flags of the regular expression module. Use the or operator to combine flags, and the and operator to test whether one is present.",
  "walk": "We define three flags as powers of two: read as one, write as two, and execute as four. Combining read and write with the or operator gives three, which is zero b one, one in binary. Testing the read flag with the and operator gives a non-zero result, so Read permission granted prints. The same is true for write. But execute is not part of the permissions, so the else branch runs and prints Execute permission not granted."},
 {"id": "6.1", "title": "Membership Operators Overview",
  "concept": "The membership operators, in and not in, ask whether a value is found inside a collection: a list, a tuple, a set, a dictionary, or even a string. With a string, they look for a substring. With a dictionary, they check the keys. Here is a professional performance tip. On a list, Python has to look at the items one by one, so a big list is slow. On a set or a dictionary, the lookup uses a hash table and is almost instant, no matter how large it is. So if you need to check membership many times, convert your list into a set first.",
  "walk": "We create a list of three fruits. Apple in fruits is True, because apple is in the list. Grape in fruits is False. And grape not in fruits is True, since not in simply reverses the test. On a string, in looks for a substring: world is found inside Hello, world, but Python is not."},
 {"id": "7.1", "title": "Identity Operators Overview",
  "concept": "Identity operators ask a different question from comparison. The double equals asks, do these two things have the same value? The word is asks, are these two names pointing at the very same object in memory? This distinction explains one of the classic beginner bugs: when you write b equals a for a list, you do not make a copy, you make a second name for the same list, so changing one changes the other. To really copy, use a dot copy. The professional rule is simple: use is only for None, written is None, because None exists only once. Do not use it for numbers or strings. Small numbers appear to behave nicely because CPython caches them, but that is an implementation detail you should never rely on.",
  "walk": "We create a list a, then b that points at the same list, then c, a separate list with the same contents. The id function prints the memory address of each object. The exact numbers will differ on your computer, but a and b always show the same address, while c has its own. So a is b is True, a is c is False, and yet a equals c is True, because the contents are equal. Python also caches small integers, so x is y is True for the number ten."},
 {"id": "8.1", "title": "Precedence Rules",
  "concept": "Precedence decides which operator is evaluated first when several appear in one expression, just like in school mathematics. From highest to lowest: parentheses, then exponentiation, then multiplication, division, floor division and modulo, then addition and subtraction, then the bitwise operators, then comparisons, membership and identity, and finally not, and, and or. A famous trap: negative two to the power of two gives negative four, not four, because the power is evaluated before the minus sign. But here is the advice of experienced engineers. Do not memorize the whole table. When an expression is not obvious, add parentheses. The code PEP eight style guide agrees: clear beats clever.",
  "walk": "We start with a heading, then compare two arithmetic expressions. Multiplication is evaluated before addition, so ten plus five times two is twenty, not thirty. Parentheses change the order: ten plus five, in brackets, times two is thirty. Comparison operators are evaluated before logical ones, so x less than y and y less than z is True. Changing the second comparison makes the and False. And the or is True, because only one side needs to be true."},
